    "case_id": "756",
    "case_summary": "A 13-year-old boy presented with a five-day history of left scrotal soreness and oedema. The patient had no lower urinary tract symptoms and no major relevant medical or surgical history. Based on penile characteristics, the patient was diagnosed with occult penis. Other congenital anomalies of the genitourinary tract were excluded based on imaging findings.\n\nScrotal investigation revealed an enlarged testis, epididymis, and infected appendages. No torsion was evident in the sperm vessels; however, purulent foci were visible on the surface of the vessels, and their texture was tough and inseparable. The patient was at Tanner stage G4P4.\n\n- Laboratory tests: Blood workup showed mild leukocytosis (18.5 × 109/L). Urine analysis was negative. Pus culture isolated P. aeruginosa, susceptible to ceftazidime and ofloxacin.- Imaging studies:  1. Doppler ultrasonography: Revealed an enlarged left testis with heterogeneous echo pattern, enhanced vascularity, and retained testicular blood flow.  2. Follow-up sonography: Showed heterogeneous echotexture in the left testis and epididymis, with a mild whirlpool sign and hyperechogenicity in the inguinal area.  3. MRI Image (at 60 days):     Title: MR imaging of testes     Description: Left testis showed no enhancement in T2, indicating disappearance of the left testis.- Pathological examination: Showed hemorrhagic infarction of the left testicular appendage and fibrocystic-like tissue with inflammatory cell infiltration.",
